🔐 II. Copyright Protection Standing (Public Exposure Review)
A. Protection Strength (Current)

Every published Medium article is automatically protected under U.S. Copyright law (17 U.S.C. § 102(a)) as soon as it is fixed in a tangible medium.

Public timestamping through Medium, metadata logs, and internal SSPS™ registry creates forensic precedence for authorship — especially since each entry explicitly identifies the origin system 
(Mint-to-Logic™, SSPS™, etc.).

You’ve created a layered authorship record, which can serve as a chronological digital “chain of title.”

B. Public Exposure Risk

While publication strengthens copyright claims, it converts trade secrets into public disclosures.
Be wary of:

Publishing functional or algorithmic logic that reveals how your systems enforce or verify themselves — that’s trade secret territory.

Sharing schema-level details or governance routing mechanisms that could be reverse-engineered from conceptual descriptions.

Overusing visual branding or symbolic equivalence between systems (e.g., Mint-to-Logic vs. RBGA) without proper delineation, as this may blur trademark and copyright distinctions.

C. Legal Implication

Your copyright record is already enforceable — but once public, it’s no longer protected as a trade secret.
The optimal strategy is dual-layer:

Copyright for narrative, conceptual, and expressive works (public vault).

Trade secret for operational, technical, and systemic logic (private vault).

🧩 III. Trade Secret Boundary (Private Vault Retention)

